  the TPU v2 pod  now I mentioned large batches one other  issue you have to wonder about when  you're dealing with large batch training  is whether you can still make efficient  progress towards the ultimate goal  previously as people increase the batch  size at some point you would find that  you wouldn't reach the final accuracy  that you were expecting and so there was  sort of a mathematical limit and how  large you could make the batch size  before it was no longer efficient or  useful to do so but there's been a lot  of progress on this recently and there's  still very active research and what I'm  showing here is that a model that might  take you know and that that sort of  golden curve you know many hours on a  single board to get to this 76 percent  accuracy as you scale up within the pod  just increasing the batch size not  changing the code you can achieve that  same accuracy in shorter and shorter  wall clock times which really lets you  iterate faster because remember this is  like compiling your code right you need  to compile your code many times to  iterate and explore and sometimes if  you're doing something like hyper  parameter tuning or neural architecture  search you want to train hundreds or  even thousands of different models and  on these pods you can really pull it  pull that time in to get to the same  accuracy and iterate much more quickly  and make faster progress now here's just  a glimpse of our future roadmap I  mentioned the TPU v3 pod that we  announced at Google i/o and here's the  full system with these eight racks and  the whole pod as I mentioned is more  than eight times more powerful than the  TPU pod and actually to get to this  level of performance we brought liquid  cooling into our  data centers for the first time as you  can see here on one of the boards that  makes up that gigantic TPU V 3 pod so  we're really excited about continuing to  expand the frontier of machine learning  you can get started with cloud GPUs  today there's no waiting there's no sign  up there's no need to to make a quota  request every GCP project can start a  cloud GPU today and you can follow just  the link at the bottom the gqo slash  cloud TPU link to get started or one of  these other links to peruse the code of  our open source reference models or jump  straight to our documentation with that  i'd like to invite you to stay tuned for  live Q&amp;A we'll be back in less than a  minute  you  hi everyone I'm back to answer your  questions about cloud GPUs so one  question here is you know I filed a  quota request and haven't heard back yet  when will I get a chance to try cloud  GPUs the good news is as of recently you  no longer need to file a quota request  cloud TPU quota is available to everyone  so you can just go to Google cloud today  and you should find quota for at least  two cloud GPUs possibly more and you can  start one up right now in the US central  region let's see there's another  question here asking about the  performance of TP use vs. GPUs or other  platforms now as I mentioned earlier one  thing that we really recommend is that  you perform your own benchmark  measurements on the workloads that  matter to you  using ideally our reference models that  give you this great starting point but  in in any case we really emphasize that  you should benchmark with realistic  training data so you're measuring the  actual accuracy along the way not just  the samples per second and think about  the total time that it takes to get to  an accuracy if you're measuring training  or the throughput if you're measuring  inference and also the total cost on  public clouds or you know if you're  working on premise the whole capex and  OPEX to run the machines and maintain  them  to get a complete picture of how to  compare one platform to another if you  want to look at you know GPUs and GPU  specifically one of our customers Rhys  ml has published a series of blog posts  where they've looked at TPU and GPU  performance quite carefully you can also  look at the Stanford dawn bench results  let's see where can I access these  reference models so in the tensorflow  github repository at github calm slash  tensorflow  there's a directory slash TPU and a lot  of our reference models are in there as  I mentioned during the talk though there  are some of these that are also in a  different repository which is called  tensor two tensor where the two is a  numeral and it's all one word and so  keep an eye on our reference repository  in github and then also take a look at  the tensor two tensor library for the  real cutting edge of these sequence to  sequence models  this is a great question can I do  inference on TPS or only training TP you  support both training and inference at  the moment our software stack is  continuing to mature and I'd really  recommend training and batch inference  over time as we continue to optimize the  software stack TP you should be great  for low latency inference - but I would  wait just a little bit longer before  diving into that and now somebody's  asking well wait this is the second  generation of TP users their third  generation and so as I mentioned through  the talk we revealed the third  generation TPU at Google i/o it's liquid  cooled we assemble them it's a whole new  chip in a whole new system that we  assemble into much larger pods that  deliver more than a hundred peda flops  of compute collectively so we're pushing  the frontier forward as far as we can to  to enable us to Train even larger and  more powerful and accurate machine  learning models that will then you know  benefit all the users of Google products  through all our different applications  let's see if there are any other  questions here great so one thing one  other thing that you might wonder about  is how to do sort of interactive  development with these cloud CPUs and  one thing that I'd recommend would be  you know do as much as you can with  layers and estimators and the data sets  API on a cloud TPU before you think  about scaling up into a slice of a pod  so the the experience that we're really  trying to build towards is one where you  can you know inexpensively treat one of  these cloud GPUs as your personal  supercomputer in the cloud that you can  just sit on instead of having to worry  about a machine under your desk you just  log into the cloud and pay only for what  you're using as you do your development  and then when you're really ready to  scale up just by changing the batch size  suddenly you're able to transform you  know days in two hours or hours into  minutes and get a lot more done in a  day than you used to be able to do in a  week so we're building a whole new  paradigm for machine learning you know  research development and production here  with cloud GPUs and we invite all of you  to join us on this journey and try out  cloud TP use today thanks very much  stay tuned for the next session which is  make your applications fast and reliable  with the stack driver API thanks again  you
 

